ts-guide-1-title = You are inside the cycle

ts-guide-1-text = TarkovStation is about raids, loot and trade. All players share a peaceful hub. Beyond it lies the raid, where no alliance guarantees safety.

    A cycle lasts 10 hours. Characters, money and property reset at its end. Wealth and kill leaders appear in the final standings. In the story, leaders get a chance to escape the cycle.

ts-guide-1-tip = Do not expect a normal SS14 station shift. Prepare, recover valuables and return alive.

ts-guide-2-title = Character, faction and branch

ts-guide-2-text = Create a human and choose a name, faction and branch: assault, medic or engineer. Your branch sets your first equipment kit, but does not restrict other weapons or tools.

    Factions help you find teammates. In raids you can ally with anyone, change agreements or kill allies. Trust is your choice.

ts-guide-2-tip = Your first kit is issued at creation. Replacement kits are bought from the trader.

ts-guide-3-title = Finding your way around the hub

ts-guide-3-text = The hub is a shared safe area. Attacks are forbidden and blocked by hub protection. Approach the required object and interact with E or a click.

    Lom, the trader: buy equipment and sell stored items.
    Stash terminal: store equipment or take it with you.
    Raid console: destination conditions, party and readiness.
    Contract board: paid player tasks.
    Trade terminal: exchange with another player.
    Results terminal: current leaders and previous cycle results.

ts-guide-3-tip = Trade and raid windows open at their physical services. The guide button is available at the top.

ts-guide-4-title = Stash and preparation

ts-guide-4-text = The stash protects property until the cycle ends. Bring only what you are willing to risk. The carried-items list also includes backpack contents, which can be stored separately.

    To sell an item, store it first, then open Lom's selling section. Search, categories and rarity filters help find products. Bring matching ammunition, medicine, food, water and a light for dark raids.

ts-guide-4-tip = Emergency kits help impoverished players return to action and have a cooldown.

ts-guide-5-title = Deployment and events

ts-guide-5-text = The raid console previews biome, size, time of day and event. Size is independent of population: a solo player can get a large map. Daytime loot is modest; nighttime loot is richer and more dangerous.

    Invite teammates to a party for a shared landing. Everyone must ready up. Deployment follows the countdown.

    Airdrops bring valuable cargo; fauna migrations make routes dangerous. Watch for messages and sounds.

ts-guide-5-tip = One 15-minute raid runs at a time. Late entry never resets its timer, loot or events.

ts-guide-6-title = Extract alive

ts-guide-6-text = Green extraction beacons are on the east and west sides of the map. Approach and activate one. Stay close until the countdown completes. Taking damage or moving away cancels extraction.

    A successful extraction returns you to the hub with your equipment and loot. Anyone left when the timer expires loses their raid items. A raid ends early when everyone has left.

ts-guide-6-tip = Finding a valuable item is only half the job. Bring it out alive.

ts-guide-7-title = Death, money and your first step

ts-guide-7-text = Death costs everything you brought into the raid and found there. Your wallet and stash remain until the cycle resets. You return to the hub after death, but cannot enter that same raid again: wait for the next one.

    Leaderboard wealth includes money, reserved funds and stored property. Active raid gear is excluded. Player contracts pay under their stated conditions; raid proof requires a living extraction.

    Keep the main chat in character. Use LOOC for out-of-character talk. Report problems to the team through Discord.

ts-guide-7-tip = Start simple: create a character, find Lom and the stash, read the raid briefing and prepare.

